<item>
            <title>[LU-20849] lod: lod_primary_pick() should use write extent as a fallback health check</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20849</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Problem&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Problem&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;lod_primary_pick()&lt;/tt&gt; takes a write &lt;tt&gt;extent&lt;/tt&gt; but never uses it. A mirror is a first-class pick only when every inited OST on it is available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That check is too strict: one inactive OST outside the write range disqualifies the whole mirror, even though the write could succeed. The pick then falls back to a &lt;tt&gt;lme_prefer&lt;/tt&gt; or any remaining mirror that may itself contain an unavailable OST, or fails with &lt;tt&gt;ENODATA&lt;/tt&gt; when no mirror is fully healthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Whynotaplainoverlapcheck&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Why not a plain overlap check&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;code panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;codeContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;code-java&quot;&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;code-keyword&quot;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; (!lu_extent_is_overlapped(extent, &amp;amp;lod_comp-&amp;gt;llc_extent))
    &lt;span class=&quot;code-keyword&quot;&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That would replace all-OST-health with overlap-OST-health as the first-class pick.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first write on an RDONLY FLR file picks a primary and stays on it until resync. After the pick, &lt;tt&gt;lod_stale_components()&lt;/tt&gt; marks overlapping components on the other mirrors stale, and &lt;tt&gt;lme_stale&lt;/tt&gt; is mirror-wide, so those mirrors drop out. Later writes go through &lt;tt&gt;lod_declare_update_write_pending()&lt;/tt&gt;, which does not call &lt;tt&gt;lod_primary_pick()&lt;/tt&gt; again. A later write outside the first range can then hit an inactive OST ignored at pick time. Example: mirror A (preferred) is healthy on &lt;tt&gt;[0, 1MiB)&lt;/tt&gt; but has an inactive OST on &lt;tt&gt;[1MiB, EOF)&lt;/tt&gt;; mirror B is fully healthy. A write to &lt;tt&gt;[0, 64KiB)&lt;/tt&gt; would pick A, and a later write at 2MiB has nowhere else to go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also &lt;tt&gt;LAYOUT_INTENT_TRUNC&lt;/tt&gt; to 0 is &lt;tt&gt;[0, 0)&lt;/tt&gt;, which overlaps nothing and would skip the check entirely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Proposedchange&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Proposed change&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep all-OST-health first. Add overlap-health as an intermediate fallback:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;all inited OSTs available (current; prefer &lt;tt&gt;lme_prefer&lt;/tt&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;only OSTs overlapping &lt;tt&gt;extent&lt;/tt&gt; available (new; prefer &lt;tt&gt;lme_prefer&lt;/tt&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;tt&gt;lme_prefer&lt;/tt&gt; with some unavailable OST&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;any remaining non-stale, non-parity mirror&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Empty extents keep the all-OST check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Tests&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Tests&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sanity-flr: prefer a fully healthy mirror when one exists; if none, pick a mirror healthy only in the write range; trunc-to-0 still runs the all-OST check.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
